1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,107
What does it take to build a bank's e-commerce acquiring system from scratch?

2
00:00:04,107 --> 00:00:10,321
In this series we build one, part by part, and this map is where we track every step.

3
00:00:10,321 --> 00:00:18,809
On top are the three users: the merchant's server, the buyer, and the bank admin. Inside is one modular monolith with five modules.

4
00:00:18,809 --> 00:00:23,357
Partner owns the merchants: who they are, and whether they may sell.

5
00:00:23,357 --> 00:00:26,309
Commission decides the fee on every payment.

6
00:00:26,309 --> 00:00:32,357
Terminal knows which bank terminal, the MID and TID, each payment goes through.

7
00:00:32,357 --> 00:00:36,750
The card vault keeps card numbers encrypted, and hands out tokens instead.

8
00:00:36,750 --> 00:00:41,357
Payment creates every payment, debits the card, and resolves the outcome.

9
00:00:41,357 --> 00:00:46,750
At the edges, two ports talk to the outside world: email, and the processing center.

10
00:00:46,750 --> 00:00:52,988
Below sits the shared infrastructure: Postgres, Redis, Keycloak, Vault, and Temporal.

11
00:00:52,988 --> 00:00:56,571
Part zero brings that infrastructure up with a single command.

12
00:00:56,571 --> 00:01:03,261
Part one builds the merchants: sign-up, email codes, two-admin approval, and API credentials.

13
00:01:03,261 --> 00:01:08,440
Part two adds the money rules: the commission, and the terminals we debit through.

14
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:15,345
Part three handles cards: a fake processing center, the adapters, and a vault that stores only tokens.

15
00:01:15,345 --> 00:01:21,333
Part four builds the payment itself, and it comes last because it needs everything before it.

16
00:01:21,333 --> 00:01:26,345
Part five moves the card vault into its own service, to show why clean boundaries matter.

17
00:01:26,345 --> 00:01:30,642
Next, we set up the project and bring the whole world up with one command.

